{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III was a count of Tripoli who ruled from approximately 1193 to 1202 and again from 1202 to 1216. He succeeded his father Raymond II who had been a prominent figure among the Crusader states in the Levant. Raymond III was born into the powerful House of Bures and grew up during a time of shifting alliances and military conflicts in the region.\n\n### *highlighted section part 1*\nRaymond III rose to power at a young age after his father Raymond II was captured and killed in 1193 during a battle. Raymond III was held captive by Saladin’s nephew Al-Adil I for several months before being released through negotiations and ransom. Upon his return he took control of Tripoli securing the county as a vassal state within the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His reign was marked by efforts to maintain stability in the face of persistent threats from Muslim powers.\n\nInitially Raymond III relied on the help of the Knights Templar and other Crusader forces to defend his territories. The county’s geographical position along the Mediterranean coast made it a valuable but vulnerable target. He faced multiple sieges and raids led by Ayyubid forces under the command of Al-Adil and later his brother Al-Kamil. Despite these challenges Raymond III maintained Tripoli’s independence through a mix of diplomacy and military resilience.\n\n### *highlighted section part 2*\nIn the early part of his reign Raymond III struggled to establish firm authority over the city of Antioch which had a complex history of semi-autonomy. His relationship with Bohemond IV Prince of Antioch was strained by disagreements over control and resources. Raymond eventually secured a treaty with Bohemund in 1197 which allowed him to retain influence over Tripoli while recognizing Antioch’s separate governance. This diplomatic effort helped stabilize the region temporarily.\n\nRaymond III also played a key role in the Fourth Crusade though his involvement was indirect. The Crusaders’ diversion to Constantinople in 1202 and 1204 weakened the immediate threat to the Levant but also reduced the number of reinforcements and resources available to the Crusader states. Raymond III took advantage of this period to consolidate his power in Tripoli and strengthen his alliances with other regional rulers such as the Kingdom of Armenia and the Principality of Morea.\n\n### Early death and legacy\nRaymond III’s reign ended abruptly in 1202 when he died presumably due to an accident involving a falling window shutter. His death was a significant loss for the Crusader states as he had been a capable leader who managed to keep Tripoli intact during a turbulent period. He was succeeded by his son Raymond Ruben who was still a minor leading to a regency period managed by Raymond’s widow Plaisance of Antioch and his uncle Philip of Gibelet.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy lies in his ability to preserve the County of Tripoli in an era dominated by Muslim military dominance and internal Crusader divisions. His efforts to balance diplomacy and military action set a precedent for future rulers in the region. While Tripoli eventually fell to the Mamluks in 1289 Raymond III’s contributions helped delay that outcome by decades ensuring the county’s survival through a critical period in the history of the Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012694983161054552}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.004028895869851112}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -2.4148218631744385}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.44175252318382263}, {"token": " count", "logprob": -1.1469616889953613}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.10946299880743027}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.14305217564105988}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.5704081058502197}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.21429389715194702}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.31